\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The group-algebra action of the half-turn}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{The [half-turn](fdip-000J) acts on fields before a precision or
probability law is chosen. Let #{k} be a nonzero commutative ring and
#{C_2=\{1,r\}}, with #{r^2=1}. In its
\vocabk{Group Algebra}{fgap-001H}, write #{g=[r]} and #{1=[1]}.
Every element has unique coefficients #{a+bg}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
(a+bg)(c+dg)&=(ac+bd)\\
 &\quad+(ad+bc)g.
\end{aligned}}
\citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations}
gives the group basis and linear extension over a field. The following
two-element calculation works over the stated ring.}

\p{Put #{V_k=k^4}, in vertex order #{(0,1,2,3)}, and
#{W_k=k^R}, with #{R=(0,2)} in that order. Let
#{\delta_i(j)=\mathbf1_{j=i}} be the coordinate basis of #{V_k};
let #{\delta_i^R(j)=\mathbf1_{j=i}} for #{i,j\in R} be the
coordinate basis of #{W_k}. These coordinate vectors differ from the
real [spectral vectors #{u_a}](fdip-000B) and from the edge labels
#{e_i}. The half-turn and its retained action are
##{\begin{aligned}
P(\phi_0,\phi_1,\phi_2,\phi_3)&=(\phi_2,\phi_3,\phi_0,\phi_1),\\
J(x_0,x_2)&=(x_2,x_0).
\end{aligned}}
Define their unital #{k}-algebra actions by
##{\begin{aligned}
\widehat\rho_V:k[C_2]&\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_k(V_k),\\
\widehat\rho_V(a+bg)&=aI+bP,\\
\widehat\rho_W:k[C_2]&\longrightarrow\operatorname{End}_k(W_k),\\
\widehat\rho_W(a+bg)&=aI+bJ.
\end{aligned}}
The associated left module action is #{z\cdot v=\widehat\rho_V(z)v},
and similarly on #{W_k}. Both algebra actions are faithful, meaning
that their defining maps are injective.}

\p{Indeed, #{P^2=I} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
(aI+bP)(cI+dP)
 &=(ac+bd)I\\
 &\quad+(ad+bc)P.
\end{aligned}}
The same calculation uses #{J^2=I} on #{W_k}. Addition and scalar
multiplication are preserved term by term, and #{1} maps to #{I}.
These facts prove the module laws. A linear extension is unique because
#{1,g} are a basis. If #{aI+bP=0}, applying it to #{\delta_0}
gives #{a\delta_0+b\delta_2=0}; coordinates #{0,2} give
#{a=b=0}. Applying #{aI+bJ} to #{\delta_0^R} proves injectivity
on #{W_k} in the same way. No absence of zero divisors is required.}

\p{Coordinate projection
#{\pi_R(\phi)=(\phi_0,\phi_2)} sends #{\delta_0,\delta_2} to
#{\delta_0^R,\delta_2^R} and kills #{\delta_1,\delta_3}. Since
#{\pi_RP=J\pi_R}, it intertwines the whole algebra action:
##{\pi_R\widehat\rho_V(z)=\widehat\rho_W(z)\pi_R
\qquad(z\in k[C_2]).}
Thus it is a #{k[C_2]}-module map. The
[representation/module correspondence](fgap-001I) describes the
general field case; these formulas establish this particular ring case
directly. Mathlib's \leanref{Representation.asAlgebraHom} supplies
the general linear extension, and
\leanref{Representation.asAlgebraHom_single} evaluates its basis
elements. The specified #{P,J,\pi_R} supply the concrete action
and intertwiner.}

\p{Suppose now that #{2} is a unit in #{k}, and put #{h=2^{-1}}.
The element #{g} is a \vocabk{central involution}{fgap-000W}.
Its [two idempotents](fgap-000Y) are
##{e_+=h(1+g),\qquad e_-=h(1-g).}
Direct multiplication gives
##{\begin{aligned}
e_++e_-&=1,\\
e_+-e_-&=g,\\
e_\pm^2&=h^2(2\pm2g)=e_\pm,\\
e_+e_-&=e_-e_+=h^2(1-g^2)=0.
\end{aligned}}
The corresponding [two-element group-algebra product](fgap-0011) is
the concrete instance of the [central-idempotent product](fgap-0010):
##{\begin{aligned}
\Phi:k[C_2]&\longrightarrow k\times k,\\
\Phi(a+bg)&=(a+b,a-b),\\
\Phi^{-1}(x,y)&=h(x+y)+h(x-y)g.
\end{aligned}}
Substitution in either order gives the identity since #{2h=1}.
The coefficient product shows multiplicativity, scalars map to
#{(a,a)}, and the unit maps to #{(1,1)}. Thus #{\Phi} is a
#{k}-algebra isomorphism, with #{e_+,e_-} mapping to
#{(1,0),(0,1)}. This proves the product without a semisimplicity
assumption on #{k}.}

\p{The character-projector construction in
\citet{Section 4.5, p. 63}{sengupta2010representations}
assumes a field in which the group order is invertible. Here the displayed
identities give the ring-level proof. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.isIdempotentElem_halfOneAdd} supplies the
generic idempotent identity, and
\leanref/tauceti{MonoidAlgebra.cyclicTwoEquivProd} gives this exact
algebra product when #{2} is invertible. Its generator is
#{\operatorname{Multiplicative.ofAdd}(1:\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)};
#{a+bg} corresponds to the two basis monomials with coefficients
#{a,b}.}

\p{The represented projectors are
##{E_\pm=h(I\pm P),\qquad F_\pm=h(I\pm J).}
They give the [eigenspace decompositions](fgap-000Z)
##{\begin{aligned}
V_k&=V_+\oplus V_-,&V_\pm&=\operatorname{im}E_\pm,\\
W_k&=W_+\oplus W_-,&W_\pm&=\operatorname{im}F_\pm.
\end{aligned}}
For either sign #{\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}}, the concrete
\newvocab{half-turn sector} is
##{\begin{aligned}
V_\varepsilon&=\{(x,y,\varepsilon x,\varepsilon y):x,y\in k\},\\
W_\varepsilon&=\{(x,\varepsilon x):x\in k\}.
\end{aligned}}
Their free bases are
##{\begin{aligned}
b_{\varepsilon,0}&=\delta_0+\varepsilon\delta_2,\\
b_{\varepsilon,1}&=\delta_1+\varepsilon\delta_3,\\
c_\varepsilon&=\delta_0^R+\varepsilon\delta_2^R.
\end{aligned}}
The first two coordinates of #{V_\varepsilon}, and the first
coordinate of #{W_\varepsilon}, recover the coefficients uniquely.
Consequently the free ranks are two and one.}

\p{To verify the splitting, #{E_++E_-=I} gives
#{v=E_+v+E_-v}. An element in both images is fixed and annihilated
by #{E_+}, so it is zero. Further, #{PE_\varepsilon=\varepsilon
E_\varepsilon}, while #{Pv=\varepsilon v} gives
#{E_\varepsilon v=h(v+\varepsilon Pv)=v}. This identifies the
images with the displayed coordinate sets; the proof for #{W_k} is
identical. Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.IsIdempotentElem.isCompl}
supplies the abstract complement between an idempotent's range and
kernel. The coordinate bases and ranks above identify its concrete
summands here.}

\p{Projection respects these summands:
##{\pi_RE_\varepsilon=F_\varepsilon\pi_R.}
Its restriction #{\pi_\varepsilon:V_\varepsilon\to W_\varepsilon}
sends #{b_{\varepsilon,0}} to #{c_\varepsilon} and
#{b_{\varepsilon,1}} to zero. It is surjective, with
##{\ker\pi_\varepsilon=k b_{\varepsilon,1}.}
For each sign, the square commutes:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  map/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (v) at (0,0) {$V_k$};
\node (ve) at (3.4,0) {$V_\varepsilon$};
\node (w) at (0,-2) {$W_k$};
\node (we) at (3.4,-2) {$W_\varepsilon$};
\draw[map] (v) -- node[above=2mm] {$E_\varepsilon$} (ve);
\draw[map] (w) -- node[above=2mm] {$F_\varepsilon$} (we);
\draw[map] (v) -- node[left=2mm] {$\pi_R$} (w);
\draw[map] (ve) -- node[right=2mm] {$\pi_\varepsilon$} (we);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The horizontal maps select a sector; the right map removes one
free coordinate within it. The algebra factors #{k\times k},
the full rank-two sectors, and the retained rank-one sectors are
[different levels of decomposition](fgap-0012).}
